Move the touch driver to the SM8150 layout of the raphael and to its
GT9889 touch controller.

0060 was written for the sweet (SM7150) and its GT9896 touch IC, so on the
raphael every address it uses is wrong:

  * the GENI serial engine of the raphael is SE19 at 0x00c88000, not SE7 at
    0x00a84000, and it sits on QUPv3 wrapper2 instead of wrapper1;
  * the TLMM base has to be the SOUTH tile (0x03d00000), because every one of
    the touch pins of the raphael is in it.  The pin offsets themselves are
    unchanged: the MSM pinctrl driver computes them as 0x1000 * gpio, so the
    base only decides which tile a pin belongs to;
  * the clock vote register of the sweet, 0x5200c, is the PCIe auxiliary
    clock on the SM8150.  The SE19 branch lives on the wrapper2 vote
    register 0x52014;
  * the raphael powers the touch IC from two GPIOs (3V3 on gpio98 and 1V8 on
    gpio100), so the L11C RPMh vote of 0062 is not only useless, it votes for
    a regulator that does not exist on the PM660.

The halt check needs the hwcg rule as well.  gcc_qupv3_wrap_2_s_ahb_clk runs
in hwcg mode on the SM8150 and then does not report CLK_OFF in bit 31 of its
halt register; Linux skips the halt check in that case
(clk_branch_in_hwcg_mode()).  Reading bit 31 anyway is what made the driver
report "GCC branch 0x18008 halted (0x80000002)" on the raphael.

The touch IC of the raphael is a GT9889, which is a Normandy part and not a
Yellowstone one like the GT9896 of the sweet:

  * the frame lives at 0x4100, not at 0x4180;
  * the header is two bytes (status, touch count), not eight;
  * the coordinates are little endian, not big endian;
  * the checksum is the sum of every byte of the frame, which must be zero,
    not a big endian 16 bit sum at the end of the touch data.

The product id therefore has to be read from 0x4535, and the driver has to
accept "9889" rather than "9896".

The panel of the raphael is 1080x2340, so the coordinate limit is 2339 and
not 2399.

